<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<BES xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="BES.xsd">
	<Task>
		<Title>Scan with YARA-X Signatures - Linux</Title>
		<Description><![CDATA[<P>Scans this computer with <A href="https://github.com/VirusTotal/yara-x">YARA-X</A> for whatever software the deployed signatures detect, and records the version of each. It uses every rule file in the YARA engine signatures directory, <CODE>__BESData/__Global/__MitigationToolkit/yara/signatures</CODE>, in one pass; the version is read from strings compiled into the binaries, so nothing is run. Each signature file is loaded into its own YARA namespace, named after the file without its extension (<CODE>example_detect.yar</CODE> becomes namespace <CODE>example_detect</CODE>), so rule identifiers only have to be unique within one signature file. Run &quot;Deploy YARA-X Signatures - Universal&quot; first: this task is not relevant until at least one signature file is present.</P><P>Results: one line per product found in a file, &quot;signature&lt;TAB&gt;path&lt;TAB&gt;product&lt;TAB&gt;version&quot;, where signature is the signature file that matched (its namespace) and product is a short label taken from the name of the rule that matched, the text before its first underscore in lower case, so which products appear depends on which signatures are deployed (one file can give more than one line when several rules match it; a match whose rule carries no readable version string is listed as &quot;unknown&quot;), in __BESData/__Global/__MitigationToolkit/yara/results/yara_results_runtimes.text. A second file in the same folder, yara_results_signatures.text, holds &quot;signature&lt;TAB&gt;count&quot;, one line for every signature that was loaded sorted by name, counting the result lines it produced, so a signature that matched nothing is still listed with a count of 0. Activate the analysis &quot;YARA-X Scan Results - Universal&quot;, which reads both files, to report them. The task stays relevant whenever a signature file is present and no scan is running, so how often the scan repeats is set by how the action is scheduled (a reapplying or scheduled action), not by this task.</P><P>Runs as root and scans every local disk filesystem (separate /home, /var, /opt, tmpfs /tmp, snaps); network shares, FUSE mounts, container storage, snapshots, files of 512 MB or more and non-executable file types are skipped.</P><P>The scan runs in the background (started with run, so the agent stays free for other actions) from __BESData/__Global/__MitigationToolkit/yara/work and reads the whole disk, which can take 10-30 minutes or more. The yr engine is kept in the sibling bin folder and reused by later scans. The action completes when the scan starts; the results file appears when it finishes, and its console output goes to __BESData/__Global/__MitigationToolkit/yara/results/yara_scan_runtimes.log. While a scan is running the task is not relevant, so a second one is not started.</P>]]></Description>
		<Relevance><![CDATA[name of operating system as lowercase starts with "linux"]]></Relevance>
		<Relevance><![CDATA[exists (architecture of operating system) whose(it = "x86_64" OR it = "aarch64")]]></Relevance>
		<Relevance><![CDATA[exists file "/bin/bash"]]></Relevance>
		<Relevance><![CDATA[not in proxy agent context]]></Relevance>
		<Relevance><![CDATA[exists files whose(name of it as lowercase ends with ".yar" OR name of it as lowercase ends with ".yara") of folders "signatures" of folders "yara" of folders "__MitigationToolkit" of folders "__Global" of data folders of client]]></Relevance>
		<Relevance><![CDATA[not exists files whose(name of it = "scan.running" AND (now - modification time of it) < 12*hour) of folders "work" of folders "yara" of folders "__MitigationToolkit" of folders "__Global" of data folders of client]]></Relevance>
		<Category></Category>
		<DownloadSize>10347394</DownloadSize>
		<Source>Internal</Source>
		<SourceID>jgstew</SourceID>
		<SourceReleaseDate>2026-11-17</SourceReleaseDate>
		<SourceSeverity></SourceSeverity>
		<CVENames></CVENames>
		<SANSID></SANSID>
		<MIMEField>
			<Name>x-fixlet-modification-time</Name>
			<Value>Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000</Value>
		</MIMEField>
		<Domain>BESC</Domain>
		<DefaultAction ID="Action1">
			<Description>
				<PreLink>Click </PreLink>
				<Link>here</Link>
				<PostLink> to scan with the deployed YARA-X signatures.</PostLink>
			</Description>
			<ActionScript MIMEType="application/x-Fixlet-Windows-Shell"><![CDATA[
// Scan with the deployed YARA-X signatures and record what they find - Linux
// Generated by YARA/make_bigfix_content.py from bash/yara_x_scan_linux.sh in
// https://github.com/jgstew/tools - change the script there and regenerate.

// yr (YARA-X CLI) release archive for this OS/CPU; the script still checks its SHA256
begin prefetch block
	if {architecture of operating system = "aarch64"}
		add prefetch item name=yara-x-v1.22.0-aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz sha1=fde88001e579387275f731387963dd220f7a6fbd size=9252173 url=https://github.com/VirusTotal/yara-x/releases/download/v1.22.0/yara-x-v1.22.0-aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz sha256=b1b90f0686ae3e9f607c07b743fd96d6283d9ea5e2fd764919b8505de8b63d4e
	endif
	if {architecture of operating system = "x86_64"}
		add prefetch item name=yara-x-v1.22.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz sha1=f6e1f286242753e0e621c43da7f0c753c8ffc1f4 size=10347394 url=https://github.com/VirusTotal/yara-x/releases/download/v1.22.0/yara-x-v1.22.0-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz sha256=7f470770b2c1a0d97f02298fc194978d999074d147d003d33dd62775761439db
	endif
end prefetch block

// The YARA engine layout under the client data folder (the __BESData folder),
// created by "Deploy YARA-X Signatures - Universal":
//   bin         the yr binary, kept between scans
//   signatures  the .yar rule files this scan uses
//   results     the scan output read by the analysis
//   work        this scan's scripts, archive and scan.running marker
parameter "engine_folder" = "{pathname of data folders of client}/__Global/__MitigationToolkit/yara"
parameter "bin_folder" = "{parameter "engine_folder"}/bin"
parameter "signatures_folder" = "{parameter "engine_folder"}/signatures"
parameter "results_folder" = "{parameter "engine_folder"}/results"
// The scan runs in the background from here, not __Download: the client clears
// __Download when the action ends, and the scan outlives the action
parameter "temp_folder" = "{parameter "engine_folder"}/work"
parameter "results_file" = "yara_results_runtimes.text"
// Per-signature match counts, published beside the summary. Both names are
// read by the analysis "YARA-X Scan Results - Universal" - do not rename them.
parameter "signature_counts_file" = "yara_results_signatures.text"

// The deploy task creates bin and results; recreated here only if they are missing
// so a scan still runs after someone cleans the folder out
if {not exists folders "bin" of folders "yara" of folders "__MitigationToolkit" of folders "__Global" of data folders of client}
	folder create "{parameter "bin_folder"}"
endif
if {not exists folders "results" of folders "yara" of folders "__MitigationToolkit" of folders "__Global" of data folders of client}
	folder create "{parameter "results_folder"}"
endif
folder delete "{parameter "temp_folder"}"
folder create "{parameter "temp_folder"}"
copy "__Download/{name of file whose(name of it starts with "yara-x-v") of folder "__Download" of client folder of current site}" "{parameter "temp_folder"}/{name of file whose(name of it starts with "yara-x-v") of folder "__Download" of client folder of current site}"

// The scan script; every left curly brace in it is doubled, BigFix's escape for relevance substitution
delete __createfile
createfile until _END_OF_SCRIPT_
#!/usr/bin/env bash
#### Usage: ./yara_x_scan_linux.sh -r RULES_PATH [-t TARGET_PATH] [-o OUTPUT_DIR] [-b BIN_DIR] [-f text|json|ndjson] [-k] [-a YR_ARCHIVE] [-m MAX_MB] [-T THREADS] [-x DIR]... [-A]
#
# Self-contained YARA-X scan for Linux (Linux counterpart of yara_x_scan_macos.sh):
#   1. downloads the YARA-X CLI (yr) release for this CPU architecture
#   2. verifies the SHA256 of the download against a pinned value
#   3. unpacks it into -b BIN_DIR (default: a temporary folder removed on exit)
#   4. scans TARGET_PATH (recursively) with every YARA rule file in -r
#      RULES_PATH, which is a .yar/.yara file or a directory of them (searched
#      recursively), so every deployed signature is applied in one scan. Each
#      rule file is loaded into its own YARA namespace, named after the file
#      without its extension (php_detect.yar -> namespace php_detect), so rule
#      identifiers only have to be unique within one file; two rule files with
#      the same base name are a usage error. The scan takes
#      in every local disk filesystem mounted below TARGET_PATH (separate
#      /home, /var, /opt, btrfs subvolumes, snaps, tmpfs /tmp); network shares,
#      FUSE mounts (rclone, sshfs), WSL Windows drives and system-only
#      filesystems (/proc, /sys, /dev, /run) are skipped
#   5. writes results + a log to OUTPUT_DIR; for text format:
#        yara_results_<host>_<time>.raw.text  full yr output (rules, meta, strings)
#        yara_results_<host>_<time>.text      "signature<TAB>path<TAB>product<TAB>version",
#                                             one line per product in a file
#        yara_results_signatures_<host>_<time>.text
#                                             "signature<TAB>count", one line
#                                             for every loaded signature,
#                                             sorted by name, counting the
#                                             lines above it produced (a
#                                             signature that matched nothing
#                                             is listed with a count of 0)
#
#        signature is the namespace of the rule file that matched, i.e. its
#        file name without the extension. It is taken from the namespace yr
#        prints with the rule name when it prints one, and otherwise from a
#        map of rule identifier to signature built by parsing the rule files
#        for their rule declarations, so either output shape works.
#
#        product is a short label taken from the name of the rule that
#        matched: the text before its first underscore, lowercased, so which
#        products appear depends on which signatures are deployed (the one
#        exception is Apache_HTTP2_*, reported as mod_http2). One file can
#        give more than one line when several rules match it. Files matched
#        with no readable version string, e.g. Linux PHP extensions or Node
#        addons, are listed with version "unknown". Files whose names aren't valid
#        UTF-8, or whose paths contain control characters (newline, tab, ...),
#        are scanned as copies and shown as bash $'...' quoted paths (e.g.
#        $'/opt/app\351/php', $'/opt/new\nline/php').
#
# This script carries no YARA rules of its own: -r RULES_PATH is required and
# the rule files live outside it (in BigFix, deployed to the engine signatures
# directory by "Deploy YARA-X Signatures - Universal"). The rule files that
# come from YARA/ are YARA/php/php_detect.yar, YARA/node/node_detect.yar and
# YARA/apache/apache_detect.yar (each with the header check from
# YARA/is_executable.yar). The deploy task also ships rule files from
# elsewhere, such as java_detect.yar, which are maintained with that task
# rather than in YARA/. Its versions.awk filter is still not the source of
# truth: YARA/sync_rules.py copies that in from YARA/versions.awk. Edit there,
# then run
#   python3 YARA/sync_rules.py
# (CI fails if the copy here is out of date). The rest of the script is edited
# here. YARA/make_bigfix_content.py embeds the whole script in its BigFix
# task, so regenerate it after any change.
#
# Run with sudo for a full scan; otherwise unreadable files are logged and skipped.
# Needs bash, coreutils, find, grep, sed, awk, tar + gzip, and curl or wget.
# yr is statically linked, so any glibc or musl distro works (kernel 3.2+ on
# x86_64, 3.7+ on aarch64).
#
# Options:
#   -r RULES_PATH   required: a .yar/.yara rule file, or a folder of them
#                   (searched recursively); each file is loaded into its own
#                   namespace named after the file without its extension, and
#                   two files with the same base name are a usage error
#   -t TARGET_PATH  file or folder to scan            (default: / = all local disks)
#   -o OUTPUT_DIR   folder to write results into      (default: ./yara_results)
#   -b BIN_DIR      unpack yr into this folder instead of the temporary work
#                   folder, so the engine is kept and reused by later scans
#   -f FORMAT       yr output format: text|json|ndjson (default: text)
#   -k              keep the downloaded yr archive in OUTPUT_DIR, to reuse
#                   later with -a OUTPUT_DIR (its SHA256 is checked again)
#   -a YR_ARCHIVE   use a local yr release archive instead of downloading: the
#                   file, or a folder holding it under its release name (e.g. a
#                   BigFix prefetch folder); its SHA256 is still checked
#   -m MAX_MB       skip files of MAX_MB MB or larger, 0 = no limit
#                   (default: 512, or 0 with -A)
#   -T THREADS      yr scan threads                   (default: 1)
#   -x DIR          also skip this folder; repeat for more (a find -path
#                   pattern such as '*/node_modules' works too, but a
#                   filesystem mounted below a matching folder is still
#                   scanned: name its mount point with its own -x)
#   -A              no default skips: scan everything listed below as skipped
#                   by default (except empty files); -x still applies
#   -h              show this help
#
# Skipped by default (none of these can hold a likely host PHP, Node.js or
# Apache install; each skip is reported while running, files over the size cap
# and skipped mounts are listed in the log). To scan just one of these folders,
# target it (e.g. -t /var/lib/docker) instead of using -A:
#   - files of 512 MB or more: PHP builds are ~20 MB, the largest known
#     (FrankenPHP, static PHP in Go) ~180 MB, node 8.x-25.x 33-127 MB,
#     Electron frameworks 125-350 MB; this cuts VM disks, model weights, media
#   - images, media, archives, packages, documents, fonts, web/source/text
#     files, C headers, logs and databases: the rules only match executables
#     (by header); files with no extension, .so, .dll, .exe, .o are kept
#   - empty files
#   - container storage: /var/lib/docker, /var/lib/containers,
#     /var/lib/containerd, /var/lib/rancher, /var/lib/lxc, /var/lib/lxd,
#     /var/snap/lxd, ~/.local/share/containers - PHP, Node or Apache inside
#     images isn't a host install and repeats once per image layer
#   - snapshots: /.snapshots (snapper), /timeshift - full copies of the system
#     that would repeat every distro PHP, Node and Apache
# A skip whose folder contains TARGET_PATH is turned off automatically, and
# none apply when TARGET_PATH is a single file.
#
# find and yr run at low priority (nice 10, and ionice best-effort 7 where
# available) and yr uses 1 thread (-T 2 for faster scans), so a full scan doesn't take over the machine.
#
# Exit codes: 0 = scan ran, no matches; 1 = scan ran, matches found;
#             2 = usage error; 3 = download/verify/unpack error; 4 = scan error
#
# Release: https://github.com/VirusTotal/yara-x/releases/tag/v1.22.0
# SHA256 values below are the GitHub-computed asset digests for v1.22.0.
# Related: https://github.com/jgstew/tools/tree/master/YARA

set -euo pipefail

# Linux file names are raw bytes and need not be valid UTF-8. In a UTF-8 locale
# GNU grep calls such a file list "binary" and silently drops those lines, so a
# PHP at e.g. a Latin-1 path would never be scanned. The C locale keeps every byte.
export LC_ALL=C

YARAX_VERSION="1.22.0"
YARAX_BASE_URL="https://github.com/VirusTotal/yara-x/releases/download/v${{YARAX_VERSION}"
SHA256_AARCH64="b1b90f0686ae3e9f607c07b743fd96d6283d9ea5e2fd764919b8505de8b63d4e"
SHA256_X86_64="7f470770b2c1a0d97f02298fc194978d999074d147d003d33dd62775761439db"

TARGET_PATH="/"
OUTPUT_DIR="$PWD/yara_results"
# Required (-r): the rules live outside this script, see the header
RULES_PATH=""
# Where yr is unpacked (-b). Empty = a bin folder in the temporary work
# directory, removed with it. Give a lasting folder to reuse the engine.
BIN_DIR=""
OUTPUT_FORMAT="text"
KEEP_ARCHIVE=0
YR_ARCHIVE=""
MAX_MB=""
MAX_MB_SET=0
THREADS=1
EXCLUDE_DIRS=()
NO_SKIPS=0

# Extensions of files that can't start with an executable header, matched
# case-insensitively against the path end. The last line is Linux-only:
# packages, static libraries (ar archives here), git packs, journald, disk images,
# BigFix Fixlet files (.fxf, under the client's __BESData).
NONBINARY_EXT_RE='\.(jpg|jpeg|png|gif|heic|tif|tiff|bmp|webp|ico|icns|svg|svgz|mov|mp4|m4v|avi|mkv|mp3|m4a|aac|wav|aiff|caf|flac|pdf|zip|gz|tgz|xz|bz2|zst|7z|rar|dmg|pkg|xip|iso|jar|whl|docx|xlsx|pptx|doc|xls|ppt|pages|numbers|key|txt|md|rst|json|js|mjs|cjs|ts|tsx|jsx|map|css|scss|html|htm|xml|plist|strings|nib|car|py|pyc|rb|php|java|class|go|rs|c|cc|cpp|h|hpp|m|swift|yml|yaml|toml|ini|csv|tsv|log|sqlite|db|sqlite-wal|sqlite-shm|ttf|otf|woff|woff2|mo|po|lock|sum|'  # codespell:ignore caf
NONBINARY_EXT_RE+='deb|rpm|apk|tar|a|pack|idx|journal|wal|db-wal|db-shm|qcow2|img|vdi|vmdk|fxf)$'

CONTAINER_DIRS=( /var/lib/docker /var/lib/containers /var/lib/containerd /var/lib/rancher
                 /var/lib/lxc /var/lib/lxd /var/snap/lxd )
SNAPSHOT_DIRS=( /.snapshots /timeshift )

usage() {{
  awk 'NR > 1 && /^# Release:/ {{ exit } NR > 1 {{ sub(/^# ?/, ""); print }' "$0"
}

log() {{
  echo "[$(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')] $*" >&2
}

while getopts ":t:o:r:b:f:ka:m:T:x:Ah" opt; do
  case "$opt" in
    t) TARGET_PATH="$OPTARG" ;;
    o) OUTPUT_DIR="$OPTARG" ;;
    r) RULES_PATH="$OPTARG" ;;
    b) BIN_DIR="$OPTARG" ;;
    f) OUTPUT_FORMAT="$OPTARG" ;;
    k) KEEP_ARCHIVE=1 ;;
    a) YR_ARCHIVE="$OPTARG" ;;
    m) MAX_MB="$OPTARG"; MAX_MB_SET=1 ;;
    T) THREADS="$OPTARG" ;;
    x) [ "$OPTARG" = / ] && EXCLUDE_DIRS+=( / ) || EXCLUDE_DIRS+=( "${{OPTARG%/}" ) ;;
    A) NO_SKIPS=1 ;;
    h) usage; exit 0 ;;
    :) echo "Option -$OPTARG requires an argument." >&2; usage; exit 2 ;;
    \?) echo "Unknown option: -$OPTARG" >&2; usage; exit 2 ;;
  esac
done

case "$OUTPUT_FORMAT" in
  text|json|ndjson) ;;
  *) echo "Invalid format '$OUTPUT_FORMAT' (use text, json or ndjson)." >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac

# -A lifts the size cap too, unless -m is given
if [ "$MAX_MB_SET" -eq 0 ]; then
  [ "$NO_SKIPS" -eq 1 ] && MAX_MB=0 || MAX_MB=512
fi
SKIP_NONBINARY=$((1 - NO_SKIPS))
SKIP_CONTAINERS=$((1 - NO_SKIPS))
SKIP_SNAPSHOTS=$((1 - NO_SKIPS))

case "$MAX_MB" in
  ''|*[!0-9]*) echo "Invalid -m '$MAX_MB' (whole number of MB, 0 = no limit)." >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac

case "$THREADS" in
  ''|*[!0-9]*|0) echo "Invalid -T '$THREADS' (number of threads, 1 or more)." >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac

if [ "$(uname -s)" != "Linux" ]; then
  echo "This script is for Linux only (use yara_x_scan_macos.sh on macOS)." >&2
  exit 2
fi

# Minimal images (e.g. rockylinux:8) lack find; without this check a scan would
# list nothing and report "No matches"
MISSING=""
for c in find grep sed awk tar gzip sort wc mktemp; do
  command -v "$c" >/dev/null 2>&1 || MISSING="$MISSING $c"
done
if [ -n "$MISSING" ]; then
  echo "Missing required command(s):$MISSING (e.g. install findutils)" >&2
  exit 2
fi

# ---- rules ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The rules are deployed separately, so a missing or empty set is a usage
# error: scanning every file with no rules would waste hours and find nothing.
RULE_FILES=()
NAMESPACES=()
RULE_ARGS=()
if [ -z "$RULES_PATH" ]; then
  echo "No -r RULES_PATH given (a .yar/.yara file, or a folder of them)." >&2
  usage
  exit 2
fi
if [ ! -e "$RULES_PATH" ]; then
  echo "-r RULES_PATH does not exist: $RULES_PATH" >&2
  exit 2
fi
if [ -d "$RULES_PATH" ]; then
  # Sorted by full path so the namespaces, the rule map and the per-signature
  # counts come out the same on every run. find can exit non-zero on an
  # unreadable subfolder; set -o pipefail would then kill the loop, so its
  # status is dropped.
  while IFS= read -r f; do
    if [ -n "$f" ]; then RULE_FILES+=( "$f" ); fi
  done < <({{ find "$RULES_PATH" -type f \( -iname '*.yar' -o -iname '*.yara' \) 2>/dev/null || true; } | sort)
  if [ "${{#RULE_FILES[@]}" -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "No .yar/.yara rule files in -r RULES_PATH: $RULES_PATH" >&2
    exit 2
  fi
else
  RULE_FILES=( "$RULES_PATH" )
fi
RULE_COUNT="${{#RULE_FILES[@]}"

# One YARA namespace per rule file, named after the file without its
# extension (php_detect.yar -> php_detect), so a rule identifier only has to
# be unique within its own file. Two files with the same base name would
# share a namespace and bring those collisions straight back, so that is a
# usage error naming both rather than a silent pick.
for f in "${{RULE_FILES[@]}"; do
  ns="${{f##*/}"
  ns="${{ns%.*}"
  [ -n "$ns" ] || ns="${{f##*/}"  # a file named just ".yar" has no base name
  i=0
  while [ "$i" -lt "${{#NAMESPACES[@]}" ]; do
    if [ "${{NAMESPACES[$i]}" = "$ns" ]; then
      echo "Two rule files give the same namespace '$ns':" >&2
      echo "  ${{RULE_FILES[$i]}" >&2
      echo "  $f" >&2
      echo "Rename one: the namespace is the file name without its extension." >&2
      exit 2
    fi
    i=$((i + 1))
  done
  NAMESPACES+=( "$ns" )
  RULE_ARGS+=( "$ns:$f" )
done

if [ ! -e "$TARGET_PATH" ]; then
  echo "Target path does not exist: $TARGET_PATH" >&2
  exit 2
fi

case "$(uname -m)" in
  aarch64|arm64) ARCH="aarch64"; EXPECTED_SHA256="$SHA256_AARCH64" ;;
  x86_64|amd64)  ARCH="x86_64";  EXPECTED_SHA256="$SHA256_X86_64" ;;
  *) echo "Unsupported architecture: $(uname -m)" >&2; exit 2 ;;
esac

ASSET_NAME="yara-x-v${{YARAX_VERSION}-${{ARCH}-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz"
ASSET_URL="${{YARAX_BASE_URL}/${{ASSET_NAME}"

# GNU and BusyBox mktemp treat -t differently, so give a full template
if ! WORK_DIR="$(mktemp -d "${{TMPDIR:-/tmp}/yara_x_scan.XXXXXX")"; then
  echo "Cannot create a temporary folder in ${{TMPDIR:-/tmp}" >&2
  exit 3
fi
# Only the work folder: a -b BIN_DIR outside it is meant to outlive the scan
cleanup() {{ rm -rf "$WORK_DIR"; }
trap cleanup EXIT

# minimal images have no hostname command
HOST="$(hostname -s 2>/dev/null || uname -n)"
HOST="${{HOST%%.*}"

if ! mkdir -p "$OUTPUT_DIR"; then
  echo "Cannot create output folder: $OUTPUT_DIR" >&2
  exit 2
fi
TIMESTAMP="$(date '+%Y%m%d_%H%M%S')"
RESULT_BASE="${{OUTPUT_DIR}/yara_results_${{HOST}_${{TIMESTAMP}"
SUMMARY_FILE=""
# Counts per signature, written beside the summary under the same host/time
# name (yara_results_signatures_<host>_<time>.text). Text format only.
SIGNATURE_COUNT_FILE=""
if [ "$OUTPUT_FORMAT" = "text" ]; then
  RESULT_FILE="${{RESULT_BASE}.raw.text"
  SUMMARY_FILE="${{RESULT_BASE}.text"
  SIGNATURE_COUNT_FILE="${{OUTPUT_DIR}/yara_results_signatures_${{HOST}_${{TIMESTAMP}.text"
else
  RESULT_FILE="${{RESULT_BASE}.${{OUTPUT_FORMAT}"
fi
LOG_FILE="${{OUTPUT_DIR}/yara_scan_${{HOST}_${{TIMESTAMP}.log"

# ---- download -------------------------------------------------------------
DOWNLOAD_RC=0
if [ -n "$YR_ARCHIVE" ]; then
  [ -d "$YR_ARCHIVE" ] && YR_ARCHIVE="${{YR_ARCHIVE%/}/${{ASSET_NAME}"
  if [ ! -f "$YR_ARCHIVE" ]; then
    log "ERROR: yr archive not found: $YR_ARCHIVE"
    exit 3
  fi
  log "Using local archive $YR_ARCHIVE"
  cp "$YR_ARCHIVE" "${{WORK_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME}" || DOWNLOAD_RC=$?
elif command -v curl >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  log "Downloading $ASSET_URL"
  curl --fail --silent --show-error --location \
    --retry 3 --retry-delay 2 --connect-timeout 30 \
    --output "${{WORK_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME}" "$ASSET_URL" || DOWNLOAD_RC=$?
elif command -v wget >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  log "Downloading $ASSET_URL"
  wget -q -T 30 -t 3 -O "${{WORK_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME}" "$ASSET_URL" || DOWNLOAD_RC=$?
else
  log "ERROR: need curl or wget to download yr"
  exit 3
fi
if [ "$DOWNLOAD_RC" -ne 0 ]; then
  log "ERROR: download failed"
  exit 3
fi

# ---- verify ---------------------------------------------------------------
if command -v sha256sum >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  ACTUAL_SHA256="$(sha256sum "${{WORK_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME}" | awk '{{print $1}')"
else
  ACTUAL_SHA256="$(shasum -a 256 "${{WORK_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME}" | awk '{{print $1}')"
fi
if [ "$ACTUAL_SHA256" != "$EXPECTED_SHA256" ]; then
  log "ERROR: SHA256 mismatch for $ASSET_NAME"
  log "  expected: $EXPECTED_SHA256"
  log "  actual:   $ACTUAL_SHA256"
  exit 3
fi
log "SHA256 verified: $ACTUAL_SHA256"

if [ "$KEEP_ARCHIVE" -eq 1 ]; then
  if [ -n "$YR_ARCHIVE" ]; then
    log "Not keeping the archive (-k): it is already local at $YR_ARCHIVE"
  # A failed copy only costs a download next time, so it doesn't stop the scan
  elif cp "${{WORK_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME}" "${{OUTPUT_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME}"; then
    log "Kept ${{OUTPUT_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME} (reuse with -a $OUTPUT_DIR)"
  else
    log "WARNING: could not keep the archive in $OUTPUT_DIR"
  fi
fi

# ---- unpack ---------------------------------------------------------------
# -b BIN_DIR keeps yr for later scans; without it yr goes into the work folder
# and is removed with it (cleanup only deletes WORK_DIR, never a BIN_DIR
# outside it)
if [ -n "$BIN_DIR" ]; then
  YR_BIN_DIR="$BIN_DIR"
else
  YR_BIN_DIR="${{WORK_DIR}/bin"
fi
if ! mkdir -p "$YR_BIN_DIR"; then
  log "ERROR: cannot create the yr folder: $YR_BIN_DIR"
  exit 3
fi
if ! tar -xzf "${{WORK_DIR}/${{ASSET_NAME}" -C "$YR_BIN_DIR"; then
  log "ERROR: failed to unpack $ASSET_NAME"
  exit 3
fi
YR="${{YR_BIN_DIR}/yr"
if [ ! -x "$YR" ]; then
  log "ERROR: yr binary not found in archive"
  exit 3
fi
# yr is static, so this only fails on a very old kernel or a noexec TMPDIR
if ! YR_VERSION="$("$YR" --version 2>&1)"; then
  log "ERROR: yr does not run here: $YR_VERSION"
  log "  (noexec ${{TMPDIR:-/tmp}? set TMPDIR to an exec-able folder)"
  exit 3
fi
log "Unpacked $YR_VERSION"

# ---- rule files -----------------------------------------------------------
# Each rule file goes to yr as its own "NAMESPACE:PATH" argument (yr scan
# takes a list of them before the target), so the rules of one signature file
# cannot collide with another's. Logged so the result file says which rules
# produced it.
if [ -d "$RULES_PATH" ]; then
  log "Rules: every .yar/.yara file in $RULES_PATH ($RULE_COUNT file(s)), one namespace each"
else
  log "Rules: $RULES_PATH"
fi
i=0
while [ "$i" -lt "$RULE_COUNT" ]; do
  log "  namespace ${{NAMESPACES[$i]}: ${{RULE_FILES[$i]}"
  i=$((i + 1))
done

# ---- rule identifier -> signature map -------------------------------------
# Which signature file produced a match is worked out from the rule files
# themselves, by reading their rule declarations ("rule NAME", with any
# "private"/"global" qualifiers) - not from yr --print-namespace, whose exact
# layout next to --print-meta and --print-strings is undocumented.
# versions.awk reads this map to put the signature in front of every result
# line. If yr turns out to print rule names namespace-qualified, versions.awk
# takes the namespace from there instead and this map is simply unused.
RULE_MAP_RAW="${{WORK_DIR}/rule_signature_map.raw"
RULE_MAP="${{WORK_DIR}/rule_signature_map.txt"
RULE_MAP_DUPS="${{WORK_DIR}/rule_signature_dups.txt"
: >"$RULE_MAP_RAW"; : >"$RULE_MAP"; : >"$RULE_MAP_DUPS"
i=0
while [ "$i" -lt "$RULE_COUNT" ]; do
  awk -v ns="${{NAMESPACES[$i]}" -v src="${{RULE_FILES[$i]}" '
    /^[ \t]*((global|private)[ \t]+)?((global|private)[ \t]+)?rule[ \t]+[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*/ {{
      line = $0
      sub(/^[ \t]*/, "", line)
      # YARA allows "private rule", "global rule" and both together in either
      # order, so strip up to two qualifiers before the "rule" keyword. Two
      # optional groups, not an interval like {{0,2}: BSD awk has no interval
      # expressions (and braces would need BigFix doubling).
      sub(/^(global|private)[ \t]+/, "", line)
      sub(/^(global|private)[ \t]+/, "", line)
      sub(/^rule[ \t]+/, "", line)
      if (match(line, /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*/))
        print substr(line, RSTART, RLENGTH) "\t" ns "\t" src
    }' "${{RULE_FILES[$i]}" >>"$RULE_MAP_RAW"
  i=$((i + 1))
done
# One identifier in two signature files makes attribution ambiguous: the first
# file in sorted order wins and the rest are warned about, so which signature
# a result is credited to never changes silently. The same identifier twice in
# one file is just the same answer, so it is passed over.
awk -F'\t' -v dups="$RULE_MAP_DUPS" '
  !($1 in ns) {{ ns[$1] = $2; src[$1] = $3; order[++n] = $1; next }
  ns[$1] != $2 {{ print $1 "\t" src[$1] "\t" $3 > dups }
  END {{ for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) print order[i] "\t" ns[order[i]] }
' "$RULE_MAP_RAW" >"$RULE_MAP"
while IFS=$'\t' read -r rid kept other; do
  [ -n "$rid" ] || continue
  log "WARNING: rule '$rid' is in both $kept and $other; its matches are credited to $kept"
done <"$RULE_MAP_DUPS"
RULE_ID_COUNT="$(wc -l <"$RULE_MAP" | tr -d ' ')"
if [ "$RULE_ID_COUNT" -eq 0 ]; then
  log "WARNING: no rule identifiers found in the rule file(s); results will name the signature 'unknown'"
else
  log "Mapped $RULE_ID_COUNT rule identifier(s) to their signature"
fi

# ---- embedded version extractor (text format) -----------------------------
# Copied from YARA/versions.awk by YARA/sync_rules.py - edit there.
VERSIONS_AWK="${{WORK_DIR}/versions.awk"
cat > "$VERSIONS_AWK" <<'AWK_SCRIPT'
# Turn `yara -s` / `yr scan -s` output of the deployed signature files into
# "signature<TAB>path<TAB>product<TAB>version", one line per product found in
# a file:
#   yr scan -s php_detect:php/php_detect.yar node_detect:node/node_detect.yar apache_detect:apache/apache_detect.yar /opt 2>/dev/null | awk -v sigmap=map.txt -f versions.awk | sort -u
# signature is the signature file a match came from, i.e. the namespace it was
# loaded into. If yr prints the rule name namespace-qualified that namespace is
# used; otherwise it comes from -v sigmap=FILE, a "ruleid<TAB>signature" map
# built from the rule files themselves, because yr's namespace printing could
# not be confirmed here. An identifier in neither is reported as "unknown".
# Handles paths with spaces and the UTF-16 ProductVersion match ($pv), which
# yara prints with escaped NULs (8\x00.\x002\x00...). Matched files with no
# version string (e.g. Linux PHP extensions, Node addons) get "unknown".
# product is the rule identifier up to its first underscore, lowercased
# (PHP_Runtime_Version -> php, Java_Version_And_Vendor_Detector -> java), so a
# newly deployed signature file is summarised without editing this script. The
# one exception is Apache_HTTP2_*, which reports the mod_http2 module rather
# than apache.
# Versions: php from $ver / $pv; node from $ver (node.js/v...), or from $rel
# (the release URL) when no $ver matched; electron from $electron, so an
# Electron framework gets a node and an electron line; apache from $ver
# (Apache/...); mod_http2 from $_module_ver (a candidate, see apache_detect.yar).
# A rule whose strings are none of those still gets a line, with version
# "unknown".

# The map is read here, not with NR == FNR: an empty map file would make the
# first result line look like a map line. Split on the first tab only, so a
# signature name with spaces in it survives.
BEGIN {{
  if (sigmap != "") {{
    while ((getline line < sigmap) > 0) {{
      i = index(line, "\t")
      if (i > 0) rulesig[substr(line, 1, i - 1)] = substr(line, i + 1)
    }
    close(sigmap)
  }
}

# rule line: "<RULE> <path>", or with yr --print-meta "<RULE> [meta...] <path>".
# The rule token is accepted both bare (PHP_Runtime_Version) and
# namespace-qualified (php_detect:PHP_Runtime_Version). KEEP BOTH: loading each
# signature file into its own namespace may make yr print the qualified shape,
# and that could not be confirmed here. If it does and only the bare shape were
# matched, nothing at all would match and the scan would report a clean machine
# - a false negative no one could tell from a real one.
# Any rule identifier is accepted, not a fixed list of products, so a signature
# file added to the signatures folder is summarised without a change here. The
# identifier cannot start with a digit, so this never takes a string line
# ("0xOFFSET:$id: ..."), and it needs at least one identifier character, so it
# never takes a blank line.
/^([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*:)?[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*/ {{
  # The strip is applied to the identifier token only, never to the whole line:
  # a line-level cut at the first colon would eat into any path containing one.
  rid = $1; rns = ""
  if (rid ~ /^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*:[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*/) {{
    rns = rid; sub(/:.*$/, "", rns); sub(/^[^:]*:/, "", rid)
  }
  # product: the identifier up to its first underscore, lowercased; the whole
  # identifier when it has none. sub, not an interval expression - BSD awk has
  # no interval expressions (and braces would need BigFix doubling).
  prod = rid; sub(/_.*$/, "", prod); prod = tolower(prod)
  # the one special case: an Apache_HTTP2_* rule reports the module, not apache
  if (rid ~ /^Apache_HTTP2_/) prod = "mod_http2"
  # a namespace yr printed itself is authoritative; the map only answers for a
  # bare identifier
  sig = (rns != "") ? rns : (rid in rulesig) ? rulesig[rid] : "unknown"
  file = $0; sub(/^[^ ]+ /, "", file); sub(/^\[[^]]*\] /, "", file); seen[sig SUBSEP file SUBSEP prod] = 1; next
}

# string line: "0xOFFSET:$id: <matched text>"; sig/file/prod are the rule line
# this string belongs to
/:\$(ver|pv|rel|electron|_module_ver): / {{
  v = $0; gsub(/\\x00/, "", v)
  id = v; sub(/: .*/, "", id); sub(/.*\$/, "", id)
  sub(/^[^ ]+ /, "", v)
  if (!match(v, /[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+[0-9A-Za-z.+~_-]*/)) next
  v = substr(v, RSTART, RLENGTH)
  if (id == "electron") print sig "\t" file "\telectron\t" v
  else if (id == "rel") rel[sig SUBSEP file SUBSEP prod SUBSEP v] = 1
  else {{ print sig "\t" file "\t" prod "\t" v; versioned[sig SUBSEP file SUBSEP prod] = 1 }
}

END {{
  for (k in rel) {{
    split(k, a, SUBSEP)
    if (!((a[1] SUBSEP a[2] SUBSEP a[3]) in versioned)) {{
      print a[1] "\t" a[2] "\t" a[3] "\t" a[4]; fromrel[a[1] SUBSEP a[2] SUBSEP a[3]] = 1
    }
  }
  for (k in seen) {{
    split(k, a, SUBSEP)
    if (!(k in versioned) && !(k in fromrel)) print a[1] "\t" a[2] "\t" a[3] "\tunknown"
  }
}
AWK_SCRIPT

# ---- build list of files to scan ------------------------------------------
# yr has no "stay on one filesystem" option, so find builds the file list.
# Unlike macOS, Linux often spreads one system over several filesystems, so
# find gets one start point per local disk filesystem below TARGET_PATH and
# -xdev keeps each walk on its own filesystem (no network/FUSE/pseudo mounts,
# no double walks). /proc, /sys, /dev, /run, this script's own output/work
# folders (old results contain matched strings) and the default skips from the
# header are pruned. Paths are resolved with pwd -P (no symlinked prefixes).
if [ "$(id -u)" -ne 0 ]; then
  log "WARNING: not running as root - unreadable files will be skipped (see log)"
fi
# A failed cd would make set -e exit 1, which means "matches found"
OUTPUT_DIR_REAL="$(cd "$OUTPUT_DIR" && pwd -P)" || {{ log "ERROR: cannot enter $OUTPUT_DIR"; exit 2; }
WORK_DIR_REAL="$(cd "$WORK_DIR" && pwd -P)"
if [ -d "$TARGET_PATH" ]; then
  TARGET_REAL="$(cd "$TARGET_PATH" && pwd -P)" || {{ log "ERROR: cannot enter $TARGET_PATH"; exit 2; }
else
  TARGET_REAL="$(cd "$(dirname "$TARGET_PATH")" && pwd -P)/$(basename "$TARGET_PATH")" ||
    {{ log "ERROR: cannot enter the folder of $TARGET_PATH"; exit 2; }
fi
TARGET_PATH="$TARGET_REAL"

# path_within PATH DIR: PATH is DIR or somewhere below it ("/" contains everything)
path_within() {{ [ "$2" = / ] && return 0; case "$1/" in "$2"/*) return 0 ;; esac; return 1; }

# An explicitly named file is always scanned
NONEMPTY=( -size +0c )
if [ -f "$TARGET_PATH" ]; then
  MAX_MB=0; SKIP_NONBINARY=0; SKIP_CONTAINERS=0; SKIP_SNAPSHOTS=0; NONEMPTY=()
fi

if [ "$SKIP_CONTAINERS" -eq 1 ]; then
  for d in "${{CONTAINER_DIRS[@]}"; do
    path_within "$TARGET_PATH" "$d" && SKIP_CONTAINERS=0
  done
  case "$TARGET_PATH/" in */.local/share/containers/*) SKIP_CONTAINERS=0 ;; esac
fi
if [ "$SKIP_SNAPSHOTS" -eq 1 ]; then
  for d in "${{SNAPSHOT_DIRS[@]}"; do
    path_within "$TARGET_PATH" "$d" && SKIP_SNAPSHOTS=0
  done
fi

PRUNE_DIRS=( /proc /sys /dev /run "$OUTPUT_DIR_REAL" "$WORK_DIR_REAL" )
PRUNE=( -path /proc -o -path /sys -o -path /dev -o -path /run
        -o -path "$OUTPUT_DIR_REAL" -o -path "$WORK_DIR_REAL" )
if [ "$SKIP_CONTAINERS" -eq 1 ]; then
  PRUNE_DIRS+=( "${{CONTAINER_DIRS[@]}" )
  for d in "${{CONTAINER_DIRS[@]}"; do PRUNE+=( -o -path "$d" ); done
  PRUNE+=( -o -path '*/.local/share/containers' )
  log "Skipping container storage (target one with -t, or -A, to include)"
fi
if [ "$SKIP_SNAPSHOTS" -eq 1 ]; then
  PRUNE_DIRS+=( "${{SNAPSHOT_DIRS[@]}" )
  for d in "${{SNAPSHOT_DIRS[@]}"; do PRUNE+=( -o -path "$d" ); done
  log "Skipping filesystem snapshots (target one with -t, or -A, to include)"
fi
# -x: find prints resolved paths, so an existing folder is resolved like
# TARGET_PATH. A skip that is or contains TARGET_PATH would prune the whole
# walk, so like the other skips it is turned off. PRUNE_DIRS also keeps
# filesystems mounted below an excluded folder out (literal folders only).
# ${{a[@]+"${{a[@]}"}: bash before 4.4 calls an empty array unbound under set -u
for d in ${{EXCLUDE_DIRS[@]+"${{EXCLUDE_DIRS[@]}"}; do
  if [ -d "$d" ]; then d="$(cd "$d" && pwd -P)"; fi
  # $d unquoted on purpose: it may be a pattern such as */node_modules
  # shellcheck disable=SC2254
  case "$TARGET_PATH/" in ${{d%/}/*) log "Not skipping $d (-x): it contains $TARGET_PATH"; continue ;; esac
  PRUNE_DIRS+=( "$d" )
  PRUNE+=( -o -path "$d" )
  log "Skipping $d (-x)"
done

# Low priority for the walk and the scan; ionice is optional (util-linux)
LOW_PRIORITY=( nice -n 10 )
if command -v ionice >/dev/null 2>&1 && ionice -c 2 -n 7 true >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  LOW_PRIORITY+=( ionice -c 2 -n 7 )
fi

# Start points: TARGET_PATH, plus each local disk filesystem mounted below it.
# /proc/self/mountinfo: "id parent major:minor root mountpoint opts [optional...] - fstype source superopts";
# root/mountpoint escape space, tab, newline, backslash as octal (\040 ...).
ROOTS=( "$TARGET_PATH" )
if [ -d "$TARGET_PATH" ] && [ -r /proc/self/mountinfo ]; then
  MOUNTS_SKIPPED=0
  SEEN_MOUNTS=" "
  while IFS="$(printf '\t')" read -r fstype dev mroot mpoint; do
    [ "$mpoint" = "$TARGET_PATH" ] && continue
    path_within "$mpoint" "$TARGET_PATH" || continue
    [ -d "$mpoint" ] || continue  # single-file bind mounts, e.g. /etc/hosts in containers
    pruned=0
    for d in "${{PRUNE_DIRS[@]}"; do path_within "$mpoint" "$d" && pruned=1; done
    [ "$pruned" -eq 1 ] && continue
    case "$fstype" in
      ext2|ext3|ext4|xfs|btrfs|zfs|f2fs|jfs|reiserfs|bcachefs|vfat|exfat|ntfs|ntfs3) ok=1 ;;
      tmpfs)    case "$mpoint" in /tmp|/var/tmp) ok=1 ;; *) ok=0 ;; esac ;;
      squashfs) case "$mpoint" in /snap/*) ok=1 ;; *) ok=0 ;; esac ;;
      *) ok=0 ;;
    esac
    if [ "$ok" -eq 0 ]; then
      # pseudo/system filesystems are expected; log the rest (network, FUSE, ...)
      case "$fstype" in
        proc|sysfs|devtmpfs|devpts|tmpfs|ramfs|cgroup|cgroup2|securityfs|debugfs|tracefs|pstore|bpf|mqueue|hugetlbfs|configfs|fusectl|autofs|binfmt_misc|rpc_pipefs|nsfs|efivarfs|selinuxfs|overlay|squashfs) ;;
        *) echo "skipped mount ($fstype): $mpoint" >>"$LOG_FILE"; MOUNTS_SKIPPED=$((MOUNTS_SKIPPED + 1)) ;;
      esac
      continue
    fi
    # bind mounts repeat a filesystem (same device + root): walk it once
    case "$SEEN_MOUNTS" in *" $dev:$mroot "*) continue ;; esac
    SEEN_MOUNTS="$SEEN_MOUNTS$dev:$mroot "
    ROOTS+=( "$mpoint" )
  done < <(awk '
    function unesc(s) {{ gsub(/\\040/, " ", s); gsub(/\\011/, "\t", s); gsub(/\\012/, "\n", s); gsub(/\\134/, "\\", s); return s }
    {{ for (i = 7; i <= NF; i++) if ($i == "-") break
      printf "%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n", $(i + 1), $3, unesc($4), unesc($5) }' /proc/self/mountinfo)
  if [ "${{#ROOTS[@]}" -gt 1 ]; then
    log "Also scanning $(( ${{#ROOTS[@]} - 1 )) local filesystem(s) mounted below $TARGET_PATH: ${{ROOTS[*]:1}"
  fi
  if [ "$MOUNTS_SKIPPED" -gt 0 ]; then
    log "Skipped $MOUNTS_SKIPPED network/FUSE/other mount(s) (listed in $LOG_FILE)"
  fi
fi

# Files at/over the cap are printed with a SKIPPED_SIZE tag, split out below.
# "{{} \;" not "{{} +": BusyBox find silently drops "+" inside -o; only big files
# reach this, so one printf per file is cheap.
SIZE_TEST=( -print )
if [ "$MAX_MB" -gt 0 ]; then
  SIZE_TEST=( \( -size -"$((MAX_MB * 1048576))"c -print -o -exec printf 'SKIPPED_SIZE\t%s\n' {{} \; \) )
  log "Skipping files of ${{MAX_MB} MB or more (use -m 0 to include)"
fi

SCAN_LIST="${{WORK_DIR}/scan_list.txt"
FIND_LIST="${{WORK_DIR}/find_list.txt"
# A newline (or other control character) in a path would break the one-path-
# per-line lists, so such paths go NUL-separated to their own list, copied below
CTRL_LIST="${{WORK_DIR}/ctrl_list.bin"
: >"$CTRL_LIST"
log "Listing files under $TARGET_PATH"
# shellcheck disable=SC2016  # $0/$1 are for the inner sh
"${{LOW_PRIORITY[@]}" find "${{ROOTS[@]}" -xdev \( "${{PRUNE[@]}" \) -prune \
  -o -type f ${{NONEMPTY[@]+"${{NONEMPTY[@]}"} \
  \( -path '*[[:cntrl:]]*' -exec sh -c 'printf "%s\0" "$1" >>"$0" || true' "$CTRL_LIST" {{} \; \
     -o "${{SIZE_TEST[@]}" \) >"$FIND_LIST" 2>>"$LOG_FILE" || true  # non-zero on permission errors

TAB=$'\t'
SIZE_SKIPPED="$(grep -c "^SKIPPED_SIZE${{TAB}" "$FIND_LIST" || true)"
if [ "$SIZE_SKIPPED" -gt 0 ]; then
  sed -n "s/^SKIPPED_SIZE${{TAB}/skipped (>= ${{MAX_MB} MB): /p" "$FIND_LIST" >>"$LOG_FILE"
  log "Skipped $SIZE_SKIPPED file(s) of ${{MAX_MB} MB or more (listed in $LOG_FILE)"
fi
if [ "$SKIP_NONBINARY" -eq 1 ]; then
  grep -v "^SKIPPED_SIZE${{TAB}" "$FIND_LIST" | grep -Eiv "$NONBINARY_EXT_RE" >"$SCAN_LIST" || true
  NONBINARY_SKIPPED=$(( $(wc -l <"$FIND_LIST") - SIZE_SKIPPED - $(wc -l <"$SCAN_LIST") ))
  log "Skipped $NONBINARY_SKIPPED non-executable file type(s) (use -A to include)"
else
  grep -v "^SKIPPED_SIZE${{TAB}" "$FIND_LIST" >"$SCAN_LIST" || true
fi

# yr --scan-list aborts at the first path that isn't valid UTF-8 ("stream did not
# contain valid UTF-8") and still exits 0, so one odd file name would silently
# empty the whole scan. Such files, and those with control characters in their
# paths, are scanned as ASCII-named copies in the work folder (a symlink loses
# the path in json output, a hard link would change the file's ctime), and their
# results show the bash $'...' quoted real path.
UTF8_SPLIT_AWK="${{WORK_DIR}/utf8_split.awk"
cat > "$UTF8_SPLIT_AWK" <<'AWK_SCRIPT'
# needs LC_ALL=C so awk sees bytes; valid lines to stdout, others to file "bad"
BEGIN {{
  t  = "[\200-\277]"
  u8 = "^([\001-\177]|[\302-\337]" t "|\340[\240-\277]" t "|[\341-\354\356\357]" t t "|\355[\200-\237]" t "|\360[\220-\277]" t t "|[\361-\363]" t t t "|\364[\200-\217]" t t ")*$"
}
{{ if ($0 ~ u8) print; else print > bad }
AWK_SCRIPT
NONUTF8_LIST="${{WORK_DIR}/nonutf8_list.txt"
COPY_MAP="${{WORK_DIR}/copy_map.txt"
: >"$NONUTF8_LIST"; : >"$COPY_MAP"
# Pure-ASCII paths (nearly all) are valid as-is; only the rest go through awk
HIGH_BYTE=$'[\x80-\xff]'
grep -v "$HIGH_BYTE" "$SCAN_LIST" >"${{SCAN_LIST}.utf8" || true
grep "$HIGH_BYTE" "$SCAN_LIST" | awk -v bad="$NONUTF8_LIST" -f "$UTF8_SPLIT_AWK" >>"${{SCAN_LIST}.utf8" || true
mv "${{SCAN_LIST}.utf8" "$SCAN_LIST"
COPIES=0
scan_as_copy() {{
  COPIES=$((COPIES + 1))
  local copy="${{WORK_DIR_REAL}/copies/${{COPIES}_"  # trailing _ so .../1_ never matches inside .../10_
  mkdir -p "${{WORK_DIR_REAL}/copies"
  # A reflink clone (btrfs, XFS) takes no extra space; BusyBox cp has no
  # --reflink, so fall back to a plain copy
  if ! {{ cp --reflink=auto "$1" "$copy" 2>/dev/null || cp "$1" "$copy" 2>>"$LOG_FILE"; }; then
    echo "unreadable (unusual name): $(printf '%q' "$1")" >>"$LOG_FILE"
    return 0
  fi
  echo "$copy" >>"$SCAN_LIST"
  printf '%s\t%q\n' "$copy" "$1" >>"$COPY_MAP"
}
while IFS= read -r p; do scan_as_copy "$p"; done <"$NONUTF8_LIST"
# Control-character paths skipped the size cap and extension filter in find
# (a folder name with a tab sends every file below it here), so apply both now
CTRL_SKIPPED=0
shopt -s nocasematch
while IFS= read -r -d '' p; do
  if [ "$SKIP_NONBINARY" -eq 1 ] && [[ ${{p##*/} =~ $NONBINARY_EXT_RE ]]; then
    CTRL_SKIPPED=$((CTRL_SKIPPED + 1))
    continue
  fi
  if [ "$MAX_MB" -gt 0 ] && [ "$(stat -c %s "$p" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)" -ge "$((MAX_MB * 1048576))" ]; then
    echo "skipped (>= ${{MAX_MB} MB): $(printf '%q' "$p")" >>"$LOG_FILE"
    CTRL_SKIPPED=$((CTRL_SKIPPED + 1))
    continue
  fi
  scan_as_copy "$p"
done <"$CTRL_LIST"
shopt -u nocasematch
if [ "$CTRL_SKIPPED" -gt 0 ]; then
  log "Skipped $CTRL_SKIPPED more file(s) with control characters in their paths (non-executable type or over the size cap)"
fi
if [ "$COPIES" -gt 0 ]; then
  log "$COPIES file(s) with unusual names (control characters, not UTF-8) are scanned as copies; results show them as \$'...' paths"
fi
log "Found $(wc -l <"$SCAN_LIST" | tr -d ' ') file(s) to scan"

# ---- scan -----------------------------------------------------------------
log "Scanning with $THREADS thread(s) at low priority..."
log "Results: $RESULT_FILE"
SCAN_RC=0
# One "NAMESPACE:PATH" argument per rule file, then the target; RULE_ARGS is
# never empty (an empty rule set exits 2 above)
"${{LOW_PRIORITY[@]}" "$YR" scan --scan-list --print-meta --print-strings --threads "$THREADS" \
  --output-format "$OUTPUT_FORMAT" \
  "${{RULE_ARGS[@]}" "$SCAN_LIST" >"$RESULT_FILE" 2>>"$LOG_FILE" || SCAN_RC=$?

# find and yr report per-file errors (e.g. permission denied) on stderr; yr still
# scans the rest and exits 0, so non-zero means the scan itself failed
if [ "$SCAN_RC" -ne 0 ]; then
  log "ERROR: yr exited with code $SCAN_RC (see $LOG_FILE)"
  exit 4
fi
# safety net for the yr scan-list abort above (it exits 0)
if grep -q 'did not contain valid UTF-8' "$LOG_FILE" 2>/dev/null; then
  log "ERROR: yr stopped reading the file list (non-UTF-8 path); results are incomplete (see $LOG_FILE)"
  exit 4
fi

# Put the real (quoted) paths back in place of the copies
if [ -s "$COPY_MAP" ]; then
  awk -v map="$COPY_MAP" -v fmt="$OUTPUT_FORMAT" '
    BEGIN {{ FS = "\t"
            while ((getline line < map) > 0) {{
              i = index(line, "\t"); k = substr(line, 1, i - 1); v = substr(line, i + 1)
              if (fmt != "text") {{ gsub(/\\/, "\\\\", v); gsub(/"/, "\\\"", v) }  # JSON string escaping
              n++; from[n] = k; to[n] = v } }
    {{ for (j = 1; j <= n; j++)
        while ((p = index($0, from[j])) > 0) $0 = substr($0, 1, p - 1) to[j] substr($0, p + length(from[j]))
      print }' "$RESULT_FILE" >"${{RESULT_FILE}.tmp"
  mv "${{RESULT_FILE}.tmp" "$RESULT_FILE"
fi

if [ -n "$SUMMARY_FILE" ]; then
  awk -v sigmap="$RULE_MAP" -f "$VERSIONS_AWK" "$RESULT_FILE" | sort -u >"$SUMMARY_FILE"
  log "Versions: $SUMMARY_FILE ($(wc -l <"$SUMMARY_FILE" | tr -d ' ') line(s))"
  # "signature<TAB>count" for every signature that was loaded, sorted by name:
  # a signature that matched nothing is listed with a count of 0, so a reader
  # can tell "this signature found nothing" from "this signature was not
  # deployed". Counts are of lines in the summary above.
  SIGNATURE_LIST="${{WORK_DIR}/signature_list.txt"
  : >"$SIGNATURE_LIST"
  for ns in "${{NAMESPACES[@]}"; do printf '%s\n' "$ns" >>"$SIGNATURE_LIST"; done
  awk -F'\t' -v list="$SIGNATURE_LIST" '
    BEGIN {{ while ((getline s < list) > 0) if (s != "") loaded[s] = 1
            close(list) }
    {{ count[$1] += 1 }
    END {{ for (s in loaded) print s "\t" (s in count ? count[s] : 0) }
  ' "$SUMMARY_FILE" | sort >"$SIGNATURE_COUNT_FILE"
  log "Signature counts: $SIGNATURE_COUNT_FILE ($(wc -l <"$SIGNATURE_COUNT_FILE" | tr -d ' ') signature(s))"
fi

if [ -s "$LOG_FILE" ]; then
  log "$(wc -l <"$LOG_FILE" | tr -d ' ') skip/warning/error line(s) in $LOG_FILE"
else
  rm -f "$LOG_FILE"
fi

# Any output in text/ndjson mode means at least one match; json always prints an object
if [ "$OUTPUT_FORMAT" = "json" ]; then
  grep -q '"rule"\|"identifier"' "$RESULT_FILE" && MATCHED=1 || MATCHED=0
else
  [ -s "$RESULT_FILE" ] && MATCHED=1 || MATCHED=0
fi

if [ "$MATCHED" -eq 1 ]; then
  log "MATCHES FOUND - see $RESULT_FILE"
  exit 1
fi
log "No matches."
exit 0
_END_OF_SCRIPT_
copy __createfile "{parameter "temp_folder"}/yara_x_scan_linux.sh"

// The wrapper that runs it and copies the results to the destination folder
delete __createfile
createfile until _END_OF_SCRIPT_
#!/bin/bash
# Started by the BigFix task with "run": runs the scan in the background, then
# publishes the summary and the per-signature counts into the engine results
# folder. scan.running marks a scan in progress (the task is not relevant
# while it exists).
T="{parameter "temp_folder"}"
D="{parameter "results_folder"}"
B="{parameter "bin_folder"}"
R="{parameter "signatures_folder"}"
touch "$T/scan.running"
/bin/bash "$T/yara_x_scan_linux.sh" -a "$T" -b "$B" -r "$R" -o "$T/results" >"$T/scan_console.log" 2>&1
rc=$?
echo "scan exit code: $rc (0 = nothing found, 1 = found, 2 = bad arguments or no rules, 3-4 = error)" >>"$T/scan_console.log"
mkdir -p "$D"
# Only a finished scan replaces the results; written under a temp name first so
# the analysis never reads a partial file
if [ "$rc" -le 1 ]; then
  # The counts go first: a reader that picks up a fresh summary then always
  # sees counts at least as new, never counts from the scan before
  for f in "$T"/results/yara_results_signatures_*.text; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    cp "$f" "$D/.{parameter "signature_counts_file"}.tmp" && mv -f "$D/.{parameter "signature_counts_file"}.tmp" "$D/{parameter "signature_counts_file"}"
  done
  for f in "$T"/results/yara_results_*.text; do
    case "$f" in *.raw.text) continue ;; esac
    # the counts file matches this glob too, and is published above
    case "$f" in */yara_results_signatures_*) continue ;; esac
    cp "$f" "$D/.{parameter "results_file"}.tmp" && mv -f "$D/.{parameter "results_file"}.tmp" "$D/{parameter "results_file"}"
  done
fi
cp "$T/scan_console.log" "$D/yara_scan_runtimes.log"
# The archive is prefetched again by every run; yr stays in the bin folder
rm -f "$T"/yara-x-v* "$T/scan.running"
_END_OF_SCRIPT_
copy __createfile "{parameter "temp_folder"}/run_scan.sh"

// Mark the scan as running before it starts, so the task isn't relevant again
// in the meantime (the wrapper removes the marker when the scan ends)
delete __createfile
createfile until _END_OF_SCRIPT_
started by action {id of action}
_END_OF_SCRIPT_
copy __createfile "{parameter "temp_folder"}/scan.running"

// Start the scan without waiting (it can take 10-30+ minutes): the action ends
// here and the client runs other actions. The results show up when it finishes.
run /bin/bash "{parameter "temp_folder"}/run_scan.sh"
]]></ActionScript>
			<SuccessCriteria Option="RunToCompletion"></SuccessCriteria>
		</DefaultAction>
	</Task>
</BES>
